#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046644} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511628} :activity/id #uuid "c82e0f22-6e6f-5ddc-90a0-55c3a1ddea8c" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048304 :topic/id #uuid "92d07a33-6647-5dd0-9be4-64be21e6041d" :topic/math-academy-id 3950} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511588 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511539 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2d0f9bd4-1c42-518e-8a85-6fd510f818c7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510874} {:answer/id #uuid "2e7b5dd1-45ce-5207-ba2f-a7fe58afd9ca" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510880} {:answer/id #uuid "a6cd4858-b628-5fda-bb17-ebfb005faec1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511134} {:answer/id #uuid "ce91986b-ea40-5ee9-8cff-03eb4608ba97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511210} {:answer/id #uuid "fa544d09-b82a-5a0e-a5ce-9b72cfaee527" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511296}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510874} :answer-field/id #uuid "4633a0d6-26c7-541d-b8e0-d9639f7db195" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511348}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a8228bcb-8e29-55e3-8d2e-2b38cb08cc3b" :question/math-academy-id "q-149498" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [5,10],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (5,10).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[5,10]$ using a right Riemann sum. Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)<0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum overestimates the true area." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is false. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is false. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{3})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(5,10),$ we have \n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{3}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{3{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{3{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{3}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{3}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\""} :step/id #uuid "0ed82c2f-3101-544e-b938-9685c699089a" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511642}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511618 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511579 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c930864-9f25-5293-a719-9a80599eef5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "30" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510804}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510804} :answer-field/id #uuid "b068ad65-9909-5808-87be-751b4ea3f48f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511409}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "f43ac4b9-2815-5dee-ad9d-013b2abdd370" :question/math-academy-id "q-346317" :question/problem "The arc length of a smooth curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ and its derivative ${f}^{′}(x)$ over the closed interval $[-4,2].$\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $\\frac{7}{2}$ | $3$ | $\\frac{9}{2}$ | $4$ |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the arc length $L$ of the curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2.$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, we have that the arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=-4$ and $x=2$ is given by\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-4}^{2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{2-(-4)}{3}=2.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nNow, we set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $-4$ | $-2$ | $0$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${f}^{′}(x)$ | $\\sqrt{63}$ | $\\sqrt{15}$ | $\\sqrt{8}$ | $\\sqrt{48}$ |\n| $g(x)=\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}$ | $8$ | $4$ | $3$ | $7$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our arc length:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-4}^{2}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈(g(-4)+g(-2)+g(0))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =(8+4+3)\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =15\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =30\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "48ceae03-b320-5028-a92a-40ef95f39f8d"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511628 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511578 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29e0ebeb-fefa-5de4-b8b4-c14ae210cc54" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "96\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510870} {:answer/id #uuid "504c6583-6b60-5cc7-a4f8-49752ce0621b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "76\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510954} {:answer/id #uuid "8bfc6107-bba4-5df6-bb3d-40aae12b2fd0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "48\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511073} {:answer/id #uuid "d2a5fb43-9733-54d4-ae70-87014652f121" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "104\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511217} {:answer/id #uuid "f127f49a-4ab2-5bca-8efb-9ad5241da47a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "52\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511277}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511217} :answer-field/id #uuid "1e36c481-1a7d-539b-a5da-9e256e0e030d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511327}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "f302fed8-a5b6-5ab4-978d-2dce7c91e287" :question/math-academy-id "q-149437" :question/problem "The table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[0,12].$ \nUsing a right Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis.\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n\n*Hint: The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by*\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=0$ and $x=12$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{12-0}{3}=4.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *right* Riemann sum with $3$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $0$ | $4$ | $8$ | $12$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $3\\sqrt{2}$ | $0$ | $2\\sqrt{2}$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $18$ | $0$ | $8$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{0}^{12}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(4)+g(8)+g(12))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (18+0+8)\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 26\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =104\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "715fe870-9c23-5686-9db6-2f539d5d9cb7" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511588}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511642 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511563 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "28b4554c-46dc-510f-ac58-1873cdfd838c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510865} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3204e2-55cf-5d83-b42c-28d5493becc3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510915} {:answer/id #uuid "6f44229b-81f7-51d2-a925-37e447f0f037" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511013} {:answer/id #uuid "8475dc1b-d081-5b20-ad32-695e8ecb76fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511054} {:answer/id #uuid "fa9f2869-4ec7-5467-98e1-21e24df7e12a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511297}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511297} :answer-field/id #uuid "afc9afa2-0355-55d7-ab22-0b155eda768a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511408}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "dc84fa2c-48fe-510a-8646-e9ae7a753f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-149499" :question/problem "Let $f(x)$ be positive and strictly increasing for $x\\in [2,6],$ and differentiable for $x\\in (2,6).$ Suppose we approximate the area under the curve $y=g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)$ over the interval $[2,6]$ using a right Riemann sum.  Given that $g(x)>0,$ which of the following statements must be true?\n\nI. ${f}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nII. ${g}^{′}(x)>0$\n\nIII. The Riemann sum underestimates the true area." :question/worked-solution "We're given that $g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x)>0.$ Now, recall the following:\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly increasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\n- If $g(x)$ is strictly decreasing, the right Riemann sum gives an *underestimate* of the area under the curve $y=g(x).$\n\nLet's now consider each statement in turn:\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $f(x)$ is strictly increasing, we have ${f}^{′}(x)>0.$\n\n- Statement II is true. Differentiating $g(x)={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{2})^{′}\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2x\\cdot f(x)+{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, since $f(x)$ is positive and strictly increasing over the interval $(2,6),$ we have \n\n$$\n2x>0,\\,f(x)>0,\\,{x}^{2}>0,\\,{f}^{′}(x)>0.\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain\n\n$$\n{g}^{′}(x)={{2x}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{>0}\\cdot {{{f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}={{2x\\cdot f(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}+{{{x}^{2}\\cdot {f}^{′}(x)}_{⏟}}_{>0}>0.\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since ${g}^{′}(x)>0,$ the function $g(x)$ is strictly increasing. Therefore, the right Riemann sum gives an *overestimate* of the area under the curve $y={x}^{2}\\cdot f(x).$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""} :step/id #uuid "9982e8c2-d630-5fcb-a2a6-f49134260361" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511646}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511646 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511455 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5e1dfa21-2b09-596a-bddf-9846939710c2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "39\\pi" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510980}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186510980} :answer-field/id #uuid "2a18cebd-84e4-5a3a-96a0-241925ce8033" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186511335}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "0272db1e-3587-51d4-ba80-cbc263d01f1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-346315" :question/problem "The volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=a$ and $x=b$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{a}^{b}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nThe table below gives the values of a positive, continuous function $f(x)$ over the closed interval $[5,20].$ \n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n\nUsing a left Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals, estimate the volume $V$ of the solid generated when the region bounded by the curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis. Do not round the answer.\n\n$V≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "Using the hint, the volume of the solid generated when the region bounded by a curve $y=f(x),$ the $x$-axis, and the vertical lines $x=5$ and $x=20$ is rotated $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis is given by\n\n$$\nV=\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}[f(x)]^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nLet $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}.$ The (regular) step size $Δx$ is given by \n\n$$\nΔx=\\frac{20-5}{5}=3.\n$$\n\nWe can approximate the integral above using a *left* Riemann sum with $5$ equal subintervals and step size $Δx$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nWe set up a table with values of $g(x)$ at each point:\n\n| $x$ | $5$ | $8$ | $11$ | $14$ | $17$ | $20$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $1$ | $2$ | $1$ | $\\sqrt{3}$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| $g(x)={(f(x))}^{2}$ | $1$ | $4$ | $1$ | $3$ | $4$ | $9$ |\n\nFinally, we obtain the following approximation for our volume:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}V & =\\pi {\\int }_{5}^{20}g(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ≈\\pi \\cdot (g(5)+g(8)+g(11)+g(14)+g(17))\\cdot Δx \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot (1+4+1+3+4)\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =\\pi \\cdot 13\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =39\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "a2816dcc-c5bf-5d5d-91a7-508f9fb5354d" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186511618}}] :activity/title "Approximating Areas Under Graphs of Composite Functions" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186510771}]}
